## Phase 2 — yeast235 results (COMPLETE, see yeast235.run.log)

All gates green:
- source compare vs syng/yeast.fa (records reversed): BYTE-EXACT, all
  3,336,986,759 bytes (one forward pass).
- SA reconstruction: all 98,541 member-11 anchor values == SA[tailrow];
  sum(SA) == n(n-1)/2.
- Orbit machinery reproduces the banked lane EXACTLY: member-11 walk over
  all rows mean=221,572.8 / max=6,516,844 (banked 221,573.0 / 6,516,845).
- Benchmark: 2,000-7,647 located rows per L, 0 SA-parity mismatches,
  0 context mismatches.

THE CENTRAL MEASUREMENT (state-valid sampling premise test):
- |V_20| = 98.50% of n; |V_39| = 97.70%; |V_100| = 95.84%. On a pangenome
  essentially EVERY row is a backward-search-reachable locate start for
  MEMs of length >= 20-100 (LCPs are huge). The state-valid restriction is
  nearly EMPTY here — it cannot shrink the anchor set much.
- Confirmed by placement: at the member-11 anchor count (98,541), the
  state-valid greedy (L=20) gives D*=31,400 / mean walk 43,160 / max
  2,164,858; the workload-independent ALL-ROWS orbit-aware greedy gives
  D*=31,665 / mean 42,844 / max 2,164,858 — IDENTICAL within noise.
  State-validity contributes ~0% on yeast.

WHAT ACTUALLY WINS (and it is big, at zero extra space): ORBIT-AWARE
anchor placement. member-11 anchors every 1024th run BY RUN INDEX, which is
blind to the LF orbit; the orbit gaps between consecutive member-11
anchored tails are permutation-random (mean 33.8k, max 6.5M steps).
Placing the SAME 98,540 anchors to bound the orbit walk instead:
- locate walk mean 210k-221k -> 40.5k-45.0k steps (5.0-5.2x)
- max 6.1M -> 1.5M-1.9M (3.2-3.4x)
- measured latency (31 threads, same run): 112-124 ms -> 21.4-24.8 ms
  per locate (5.0-5.23x)
Tradeoff curve (state-valid ~= all-rows on yeast, L=20):
  D=1,024    -> 2,089,653 anchors (21x member-11 space), mean 33,219
  D=4,096    ->   655,453 anchors (6.6x), mean 33,900
  D=16,384   ->   181,900 anchors (1.8x), mean 37,770
  D=65,536   ->    48,855 anchors (0.50x, HALF member-11 space), mean 56,475
  D=262,144  ->    12,911 anchors (0.13x), mean 141,084 (still 1.6x faster
                                                      than member-11 today)
  D=1,048,576->     3,271 anchors (0.033x), mean 514,912 (2.3x slower)
So: half the anchor space buys 4x; 7.6x less space still beats today's
latency. Anchors are u64 tail-SA values (member 11 = 788,344 B on yeast),
so these are sub-MB-to-8MB decisions, but the same curve shape will apply
at pile scale where member 11 is 3.1 MB and, more importantly, where the
WALK cost (not the anchor bytes) is the product's locate latency.

Caveat (honest): singleton-occurrence MEM starts are NOT in V_L (16-43 of
~7,200-7,600 benchmark rows, 0.2-0.6%); their walks under the state-valid
set are unbounded by construction (measured mean 12-17k steps — luck, not
a bound). The all-rows variant has no such hazard and costs the same on
yeast.

Bug history (this run): (1) anchor segments processed in run-index order
are NOT orbit order — fixed by sorting segments by orbit start;
(2) per-gap valid accumulators were not reset at gap close (cumulated
across segments) — fixed + new partition gate (sum tcnt == |V_L|).
Both were caught by internal-consistency gates before any conclusion was
drawn; the banked-lane cross-check (221,572.8) was the anchor truth.

